{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "number": 38,
  "title": "Prop 38 \u2014 Medical research bonds: potential discoveries, certain debt?",
  "fps": 30,
  "frames": 5221,
  "checked": "2026-10-19",
  "narration": "asset://ba3dab8d6d76562e34abf169f68873ece3888d9dd568c41e4d05933586460c75.wav",
  "prompter_words": "words-global.json",
  "script": [
    {
      "name": "The big picture",
      "narration": "Proposition thirty-eight asks California to borrow eight point four billion dollars for immunology and immunotherapy research. Immunology studies the immune system; immunotherapy aims to use it to fight disease. This funds research, not a guaranteed new treatment. Universities, businesses, and governments already fund medical research. Many projects never become approved treatments. A research bond supplies money now, while the state repays the borrowing with interest over time."
    },
    {
      "name": "An illustrative example",
      "narration": "Imagine a scientist studying immune cells. A promising laboratory result still needs testing for safety and effectiveness. Some ideas fail, and successful ones can take years. Funding more work can create opportunities without letting anyone honestly promise when a patient will benefit. Research teams need facilities, people, and sustained funding."
    },
    {
      "name": "How it would work",
      "narration": "About half the money would go to one qualifying research institute selected by the state. The other half would support competitive grants through a council of research institutions. Reporting and financial audits are required. At least four point two billion dollars must support research on cancer, heart disease, and Alzheimer\u2019s disease. Treatments developed with the funding generally must be offered at a twenty percent discount in California. A discount matters only if a treatment reaches patients. The analyst estimates five hundred to six hundred million dollars a year in General Fund repayments for about twenty years. Revenue from discoveries could offset costs, but its amount and timing are uncertain and could take decades."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote yes",
      "narration": "A voter might see long-term public research as worth supporting even when returns are uncertain. Discoveries could improve lives and build scientific capacity. They may accept borrowing because waiting for annual funding also has an opportunity cost."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote no",
      "narration": "You might ask whether this is the best research investment. Another voter could object to the debt, the concentration of funding in one institute, or committing money before knowing results. They might favor broader competition or other health priorities. The possibility of royalties should not be treated as a promise that the bond pays for itself."
    },
    {
      "name": "What polling indicates",
      "narration": "In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 55 percent said yes, 42 percent no, and 3 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not votes already cast."
    },
    {
      "name": "Make your own decision",
      "narration": "Yes authorizes this research borrowing; no rejects this bond without ending existing medical research. Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links, and official voter guide. Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice."
    }
  ],
